      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm having an issue with this vba macro for our monthly reconciliation. every time i run it it gives me \"run-time error '1004': application-defined or object-defined error\" on this line:\n\nSub MonthlyRecon()\n    Dim ws As Worksheet\n    Set ws = ThisWorkbook.Sheets(\"Recon_Report\")\n    ws.Range(\"A1:Z1\").Value = \"Monthly Reconciliation Report\"\n    ws.Range(\"A1:Z1\").Font.Bold = True\n    ws.Columns(\"A:Z\").AutoFit\nEnd Sub\n\ncan u tell me why this is happening? its urgent.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The error \"Run-time error '1004'\" usually means VBA is trying to do something it can't find or access, but in your specific code, there are no obvious syntax errors. It's likely that the sheet named \"Recon_Report\" doesn't exist in the workbook, or the workbook is open in a different instance, or perhaps the workbook is protected.\n\nTo fix this, you need to ensure the sheet exists before trying to set it. You can add a check using `On Error Resume Next` or simply verify the sheet name. Also, make sure the workbook isn't protected, as that can sometimes cause these cryptic errors.\n\nHere is a slightly modified version of your code that adds a safety check and handles the error more gracefully:\n\n```vba\nSub MonthlyRecon()\n    Dim ws As Worksheet\n    On Error Resume Next ' Ignore errors temporarily\n    Set ws = ThisWorkbook.Sheets(\"Recon_Report\")\n    If ws Is Nothing Then\n        MsgBox \"Error: Sheet 'Recon_Report' not found! Please check your workbook.\", vbCritical\n        Exit Sub\n    End If\n    On Error GoTo 0 ' Re-enable error handling\n    \n    ws.Range",
